BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Special Program: Historical Walking Tour of Moss Park and Corktown
  featuring Toronto History Walks
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260604T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260604T150000
UID:69fe4a011773192800186e74@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:*** The meetup location for this program is at the front of\n3
 39 Queen Street East\n(Queen/Parliament) ***\nA walk through Corktown and 
 Moss Park reveals two of Toronto’s oldest and most resilient neighbourho
 ods\, a place shaped by immigration\, industry\, hardship\, and community 
 spirit. Named after the many Irish immigrants who arrived from County Cork
  in the mid-1800s\, the area became home to railway workers\, and families
  seeking opportunity in a rapidly growing city. The neighbourhood grew up 
 alongside factories\, breweries\, and rail corridors\, giving it a rough-e
 dged but deeply human character that still lingers. Along the walk\, visit
 ors encounter reminders of old Toronto everywhere — historic workers’ 
 cottages\, churches\, former industrial buildings\, and stories of the ord
 inary people who built the city through difficult times. Landmarks such as
  Little Trinity Church and nearby Distillery Historic District help connec
 t the area’s immigrant and industrial past to the Toronto we know today.
LOCATION:St. Lawrence\, Meet at 339 Queen Street East on sidewalk
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69fe4a011773192800186e74
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tea & Entertainment
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260529T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260529T160000
UID:69e1381c8ddc1caf5f1d63cf@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Enjoy watching DVDs in the Library Auditorium. Coffee\, tea an
 d cookies or snacks will be served after the film.\nIngredient lists will 
 be available upon request.\nLife Story: First Steps\nDavid Attenborough in
 troduces the perilous beginnings of animal life\, from barnacle geese leap
 ing off 400-foot cliffs in Greenland to a humpback whale mother seeking pr
 otection for her exhausted calf in a behavior never filmed before. From pl
 ayful young fur seals training in New Zealand rainforests to vulnerable li
 on cubs\, mantids\, jerboas\, and albatross chicks\, the episode reveals h
 ow survival starts with extraordinary risks. Closed captions. 50 minutes. 
 2015
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69e1381c8ddc1caf5f1d63cf
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
