DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260602T193000
UID:69f3a95c24bd39b0b5e907a4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:AI technologies have become increasingly prevalent in many asp
 ects of modern life\, including healthcare\, finance\, transportation\, an
 d entertainment. Some examples of AI applications include virtual assistan
 ts like Siri and Alexa\, self-driving cars\, fraud detection systems\, and
  personalized recommendations in online shopping. Come join this beginner 
 in-person class to learn what AI is\, how it learns and predicts outcomes\
 , considerations when using GenAI applications\, and how the Internet of T
 hings (IOT) applies to AI.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal i
 nformation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under t
 he Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informat
 ion will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and ev
 aluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be 
 directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 
 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:St. James Town\, Front Study Area
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f3a95c24bd39b0b5e907a4
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Winter Light: The Memoir of a Child of Holocaust Survivors
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260609T150000
UID:69d6d5332866a5b488475bb1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Winter Light\n: The Memoir of a Child of Holocaust Survivors i
 s about a sequence of serendipitous rescues—an emotional rags-to-riches 
 story.\nGrace Feuerverger\, professor emerita of the University of Toronto
 \, will share her story of hope and gratitude which adds a radically diffe
 rent piece to the mosaic of post-Holocaust literature. At the core of her 
 memoir stands a little girl struggling to find a path toward the Life forc
 e and to escape from the demons of despair that permeated her home.
LOCATION:Barbara Frum\, Located in Room B on the third floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69d6d5332866a5b488475bb1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Access to Housing
